{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"arty-larval-titmouse","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`run_sweep`'s FALLBACK arm — `REBAR_ROOT` unset, root resolved from git-toplevel-of-cwd — has no seam assertion in the repository suite. The in-repo test covering it is **end-to-end only**, and measurement on 2026-08-28 showed it **survives the `repo_root=None` mutant**: the end-to-end outcome is accidentally correct even when the resolved root is discarded.\n\nOnly a seam test kills that mutant, and the existing seam test exercises the `REBAR_ROOT` arm, not the fallback. So the fallback arm's correctness is real behaviour but unpinned: a regression there would not fail the suite.\n\n## Why this is worth a ticket\n\nThis is the never-ran / vacuous class. The test's passing state is also reachable when the behaviour under test never ran, so it reports coverage it does not provide. That matters here specifically because the fallback arm is what makes the merged design's envelope wider than its alternatives — it is correct when `REBAR_ROOT` is unset provided cwd is inside the checkout — and that property is currently protected by nothing.\n\nContext: `scathing-custommade-bobcat` (6bf1-326b-7404-4034), merged as `c68cc1de3185`. The fallback behaviour WAS verified out-of-band during adjudication, against a real ephemeral worktree whose `.tickets-tracker` symlinks into the main checkout, anchored via `_proc.detached_child_cwd`; that verification lives only in a ticket comment.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A seam assertion pins the root `run_sweep` hands `compact_all_cli` on the FALLBACK arm (`REBAR_ROOT` unset, real symlinked store, real detached-child anchor).\n- [ ] That assertion kills the `repo_root=None` mutant AND the `repo_root=os.path.dirname(tracker)` mutant.\n- [ ] The existing end-to-end AC#4 test is retained or replaced deliberately, with the reason recorded — it is not deleted merely because it is weak.\n- [ ] The test uses a real worktree/symlink topology rather than a mock that cannot distinguish the arms.\n\n## Testing\n\n- RED first: add the seam assertion and observe it fail against each seeded mutant before any fix.\n- Mutation-check both mutants explicitly, checking the return code rather than eyeballing output, and confirm each seed actually applied.\n- Confirm the contrast is discriminating: pivot on the HORIZON, not the threshold — the backfill arm selects any snapshot-less ticket with at least one foldable event regardless of threshold, so a threshold-pivoted contrast does not discriminate.","id":"a027-d86c-8a00-407c","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"run_sweep fallback arm is unasserted in-repo: the AC#4 test survives the repo_root=None mutant"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787958976911721001,"uuid":"6c8d9a76-2f86-496e-807a-ef93dd55fdc1"}